---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import parameters from '../../generated/parameters.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const liveParameters = JSON.stringify(parameters);
---

<Base
  title="What-if tool — Vermont School Budgets"
  description="Try out what merging or closing schools would do to school spending and home tax rates, with every step of the math shown and sourced."
>
  <h1>What-if tool</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    See every step of the math behind a district's student count and tax rate — with a source for
    each one. Change a number and watch every step change with it.
  </p>

  <form id="scenario-form">
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="parameter-mode">Which numbers to use</label>
        <!-- Options are filled in by the model tool from the build's parameter
             sets: one per live fiscal year, then the example. The whole tool
             needs JavaScript to compute anything, so an empty select with no
             script is the honest resting state. -->
        <select id="parameter-mode"></select>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="example-warning" class="notice" hidden>
      <strong>These are not Vermont's real pupil weights</strong>
      <p>
        The numbers in this mode are made-up round numbers — like a high-school weight of exactly
        2, or a low-income weight of exactly 0.5 — picked so you can check the math by hand. They
        show how the math works, not what the real answer is. Every number below is labeled
        "SYNTHETIC TEST FIXTURE — not a real law and not Vermont law," so if you copy a number out
        of this mode, it says what it is.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>How many students</h2>
    <p>
      The average number of students, by grade group, for two years. This count is called average
      daily membership. A rule in the law decides how many of those years actually count.
    </p>
    <!-- The two membership years are the selected fiscal year and the one before
         it (16 V.S.A. § 4001(7)'s two-year average). The "FYNNNN" prefixes here
         are placeholders: the model island rewrites every [data-adm-slot] span
         to the right pair whenever the year picker changes, so slot 0 is the
         earlier year and slot 1 is the selected year. -->
    <fieldset class="field-group">
      <legend><span data-adm-slot="0">FY2025</span> students by grade</legend>
      <div class="controls">
        <div class="field">
          <label for="prek-1"><span data-adm-slot="0">FY2025</span> prekindergarten</label>
          <input id="prek-1" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="10" />
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="k5-1"><span data-adm-slot="0">FY2025</span> kindergarten through grade 5</label>
          <input id="k5-1" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="100" />
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="g68-1"><span data-adm-slot="0">FY2025</span> grades 6 through 8</label>
          <input id="g68-1" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="50" />
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="g912-1"><span data-adm-slot="0">FY2025</span> grades 9 through 12</label>
          <input id="g912-1" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="50" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fieldset-total" id="fy2025-total" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </fieldset>
    <fieldset class="field-group">
      <legend><span data-adm-slot="1">FY2026</span> students by grade</legend>
      <div class="controls">
        <div class="field">
          <label for="prek-2"><span data-adm-slot="1">FY2026</span> prekindergarten</label>
          <input id="prek-2" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="20" />
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="k5-2"><span data-adm-slot="1">FY2026</span> kindergarten through grade 5</label>
          <input id="k5-2" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="200" />
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="g68-2"><span data-adm-slot="1">FY2026</span> grades 6 through 8</label>
          <input id="g68-2" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="100" />
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="g912-2"><span data-adm-slot="1">FY2026</span> grades 9 through 12</label>
          <input id="g912-2" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="100" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fieldset-total" id="fy2026-total" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </fieldset>

    <!-- The § 4010(e) hold harmless floor only binds in years the parameter file
         marks membership.hold_harmless_applies = true (FY2025 among the live
         years). The island shows this fieldset only for those years and hides it
         otherwise, so a figure that has no effect never sits on the form asking
         to be filled in. Its value feeds prior_year_weighted_membership. -->
    <fieldset class="field-group" id="prior-year-group" hidden>
      <legend>Last year's membership</legend>
      <div class="controls">
        <div class="field">
          <label for="prior-year-membership"
            >District's weighted long-term membership last year (<span data-adm-slot="0">FY2024</span>)</label
          >
          <input id="prior-year-membership" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="" />
          <p class="field-help">
            The hold harmless floor (16 V.S.A. § 4010(e)) is in force this year, so this year's
            weighted membership cannot fall below 96.5% of last year's. Enter last year's figure so
            the floor can be tested. Leave it blank if you don't have it — the result stays unknown
            rather than skipping the floor.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset class="field-group">
      <legend>Students who add extra weight</legend>
      <div class="controls">
        <div class="field">
          <label for="state-placed">State-placed students (full-time count, most recent year)</label>
          <input id="state-placed" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="0" />
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="econ">Students in families at or below 185% of the poverty line</label>
          <input id="econ" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="40" />
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="el">Students learning English</label>
          <input id="el" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="8" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <!-- Sparsity and small-school eligibility are derived from density, not
         asserted. 16 V.S.A. 4010(d)(4) sets the density bands and (d)(5)
         gates the small-school weight on 55 or fewer persons per square
         mile, so a checkbox would let the user claim a weight the statute
         would not grant. -->
    <fieldset class="field-group">
      <legend>Sparsity &amp; small schools</legend>
      <div class="controls">
        <div class="field">
          <label for="density">Persons per square mile in the district</label>
          <input id="density" type="number" min="0" step="0.1" value="30" />
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="small-school-name">Small school name (optional)</label>
          <input id="small-school-name" type="text" value="" />
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="small-school-enrollment">That school's average two-year enrollment</label>
          <input id="small-school-enrollment" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <h2>Spending and the town</h2>
    <p>
      Before the spending below is turned into a tax rate, it is spread across the district's
      students — first counted, then weighted. Here is that count, built live from the numbers above.
    </p>
    <div id="student-summary"></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="spending">Education spending ($)</label>
        <input id="spending" type="number" min="0" step="1000" value="3370000" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="cla">Common level of appraisal (how close town values are to real sale prices)</label>
        <input id="cla" type="number" min="0.1" max="2" step="0.0001" value="0.8" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Two district figures that adjust only the excess spending comparison
         (Act 183 of 2024). They are left blank because most districts have
         neither; blank means "none," so the comparison is just per pupil
         spending. Entering them changes the amount over the threshold, never the
         base spending that flows into the rate. See excessSpending in
         model/src/tax.ts and docs/excess-spending-threshold.md. -->
    <fieldset class="field-group">
      <legend>Excess spending adjustments (optional)</legend>
      <div class="controls">
        <div class="field">
          <label for="capital-reserve">Capital reserve fund draws more than 5 years old ($)</label>
          <input id="capital-reserve" type="number" min="0" step="1000" value="" />
          <p class="field-help">
            150% of this is added to the spending compared against the excess spending threshold
            (24 V.S.A. § 2804(b)). Leave blank if the district has none.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="bond-exclusion">Principal &amp; interest on bonds approved before July 1, 2024 ($)</label>
          <input id="bond-exclusion" type="number" min="0" step="1000" value="" />
          <p class="field-help">
            Excluded from the excess spending comparison (16 V.S.A. § 4001(6)(B)). This lowers only
            the amount over the threshold, not the district's base spending. Leave blank if none.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <!-- Every other field on this form is a figure the user is supposing about
         their own district, so an illustrative default is honest. This one is a
         state-determined statutory input: the statewide average district per
         pupil education spending, increased by inflation, that the Secretary of
         Education determines on or before November 15 (32 V.S.A. § 5401(12)).
         The tool never invents a value here. When the selected fiscal year has
         the Secretary's published figure recorded in its parameter file (the
         `inputs:` block, keyed statewide_average_per_pupil), the island fills
         this field from it and the user may override it; when no figure is on
         file the field stays empty, which is null, which makes excessSpending
         produce a missing_input node whose blocker names exactly which official
         figure is needed -- a null is explicable, an invented number is not. The
         `required` attribute drives the empty-state highlight (see
         .field-required in global.css) without ever fabricating a value; the
         form is JS-driven, so it never gates a native submit. -->
    <fieldset class="field-group">
      <legend>The State's figure</legend>
      <div class="controls">
        <div class="field field-required">
          <label for="statewide-avg">Statewide average spending per student (set by the Secretary of Education)</label>
          <input id="statewide-avg" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="" required aria-required="true" />
          <p class="field-help">
            Under <strong>32 V.S.A. § 5401(12)</strong>, a district's per-pupil spending above 118%
            of this statewide average counts as <em>excess spending</em>, which raises the homestead
            tax rate. The Secretary of Education determines the figure — the statewide average
            district per-pupil education spending, increased by inflation — on or before November 15
            each year, and it is published by the Vermont Agency of Education. Enter that published
            number here. When a fiscal year's figure has been recorded in the tool's parameters,
            selecting that year fills this in for you, and you can still override it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </fieldset>
  </form>

  <div class="share-tools">
    <button type="button" id="share-link">Copy shareable link</button>
    <span id="share-status" class="share-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></span>
  </div>

  <h2>Result</h2>
  <div id="summary"></div>
  <div id="blockers"></div>

  <h2>Show the work</h2>
  <p>
    Every step of the math, in order, with what it did and why. This is the tool's own work shown
    directly — not a separate write-up that could drift away from the real math.
  </p>
  <div id="walkthrough"></div>

  <h2>Laws and sources used</h2>
  <div id="citations"></div>

  <h2>What we assumed</h2>
  <p>
    The guesses this what-if starts with, and why each one starts where it does. You can change
    every one. One thing to know: when schools combine, the tool assumes <em>no</em> cuts by
    default. Any cut you see is one you chose and can see — not one the tool made for you.
  </p>
  <div id="assumptions"></div>

  <h2>What this tool will not do</h2>
  <p>
    It does not rank what-ifs, score them, or call any result a "saving." Changes are shown as
    plus-or-minus numbers, both ways. You can set busing costs to go up as well as down, because
    combining schools often makes bus routes longer. A tool that only let that number go down
    would be building in an answer instead of showing the real choices.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some things are left out on purpose and are not part of the math: one-time moving costs,
    building aid, old-debt perks, and exact bus-route planning. If a job is assumed to be combined
    but no salary is given, the tool reports the effect as unknown — not as zero.
  </p>
</Base>

<script define:vars={{ liveParameters }}>
  window.__VT_PARAMETERS__ = JSON.parse(liveParameters);
</script>

<script>
  import { initModelTool } from '../../scripts/model-tool';
  initModelTool(window.__VT_PARAMETERS__ ?? []);
</script>
